drm/vgem: give the platform device real DMA ops on arm64

vgem registers a bare platform device with platform_device_register_simple().
That device never goes through of_dma_configure(), so arch_setup_dma_ops()
is never called for it and on arm64 4.19 it keeps dummy_dma_ops, whose
map_sg always returns 0.

Importing a dma-buf through DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_FD_TO_HANDLE attaches the
buffer to this device and maps it. With an exporter that calls
dma_map_sg() (system_heap_lite) the map fails and the import returns
ENOMEM, which Mesa reports as "zink: failed drmPrimeFDToHandle Cannot
allocate memory". Windows that need a GEM handle for an exported
buffer then never get one.

Call arch_setup_dma_ops() once, before the DMA mask is set. The device
has no IOMMU group, so this only installs the standard swiotlb ops.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.c
index 8920770d1..ec3ccbf8b 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/vgem/vgem_drv.c
@@ -450,6 +450,15 @@ static int __init vgem_init(void)
 		goto out_free;
 	}
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_ARM64
+	/*
+	 * A bare platform device never goes through of_dma_configure(), so on
+	 * arm64 it keeps dummy_dma_ops and every dma_map_sg() on it returns 0.
+	 * Importing a dma-buf (drmPrimeFDToHandle) maps it for this device, so
+	 * give the device the normal arm64 ops first.
+	 */
+	arch_setup_dma_ops(&vgem_device->platform->dev, 0, 0, NULL, false);
+#endif
 	dma_coerce_mask_and_coherent(&vgem_device->platform->dev,
 				     DMA_BIT_MASK(64));
 	ret = drm_dev_init(&vgem_device->drm, &vgem_driver,
